Fixes #6184 

I don't know how to add a regression test for this, but I tested locally on the following example:
```java
class Scratch {
    interface Foo {
        InputStream getStream();
    }
    void process(Foo f) {
        m(f.getStream());
    }
    void m(InputStream s) {}
}
```
Before this change I got an error message:
```
error: [required.method.not.called] @MustCall method close may not have been invoked on temp-var-1 or any of its aliases.
        m(f.getStream());
                     ^
  The type of object is: java.io.InputStream.
  Reason for going out of scope: regular method exit
```
After the change, I get:
```
error: [required.method.not.called] @MustCall method close may not have been invoked on f.getStream() or any of its aliases.
        m(f.getStream());
                     ^
  The type of object is: java.io.InputStream.
  Reason for going out of scope: regular method exit
```
The fix relies on the assumption that Checker Framework will never create temporary variables with names that are valid Java identifiers, which is admittedly hard to guarantee.  But, a more proper fix, which added state to indicate which variables are temporaries / synthetic, would be significantly more intrusive, and IMO not worth the additional trouble over this approach, particularly since this only impacts error message formatting.
